skill: Verbal Ability

@ Synonyms | B
Choose the word closest in meaning to RAPID.
= Quick
- Slow
- Heavy
- Quiet
? Rapid means fast, and quick is its closest synonym.

@ Synonyms | B
Choose the word closest in meaning to BEGIN.
= Commence
- Conclude
- Delay
- Hide
? Commence means to start.

@ Synonyms | I
What does EPHEMERAL mean?
= Lasting for a very short time
- Extremely large
- Very expensive
- Easy to understand
? Ephemeral describes something short-lived, such as a mayfly or a passing trend.

@ Synonyms | I
Choose the word closest in meaning to CANDID.
= Frank
- Secretive
- Angry
- Shy
? A candid person speaks honestly and openly.

@ Synonyms | I
Choose the word closest in meaning to METICULOUS.
= Very careful about details
- Careless
- Fast
- Generous
? Meticulous means showing great attention to detail.

@ Synonyms | A
What does OBSOLETE mean?
= No longer in use or out of date
- Newly invented
- Extremely valuable
- Hard to find
? An obsolete technology has been replaced by something newer.

@ Synonyms | A
What does UBIQUITOUS mean?
= Present or found everywhere
- Very rare
- Hidden from view
- Extremely loud
? Smartphones are often described as ubiquitous in modern life.

@ Antonyms | B
Choose the word opposite in meaning to ANCIENT.
= Modern
- Old
- Historic
- Aged
? Ancient means very old, so modern is the opposite.

@ Antonyms | B
Choose the word opposite in meaning to GENEROUS.
= Stingy
- Kind
- Helpful
- Wealthy
? A generous person gives freely, while a stingy person does not.

@ Antonyms | I
Choose the word opposite in meaning to EXPAND.
= Contract
- Grow
- Stretch
- Spread
? To expand is to become larger and to contract is to become smaller.

@ Antonyms | I
Choose the word opposite in meaning to VICTORY.
= Defeat
- Triumph
- Success
- Win
? Victory means winning and defeat means losing.

@ Antonyms | A
Choose the word opposite in meaning to BENEVOLENT.
= Malevolent
- Charitable
- Kind
- Gracious
? Benevolent means well-meaning and malevolent means wishing harm to others.

@ Antonyms | A
Choose the word opposite in meaning to VERBOSE.
= Concise
- Wordy
- Talkative
- Lengthy
? Verbose means using more words than necessary, and concise means brief and clear.

@ Grammar and Usage | B
Which sentence is grammatically correct?
= She doesn't like tea.
- She don't like tea.
- She not like tea.
- She doesn't likes tea.
? With a third-person singular subject use doesn't followed by the base verb.

@ Grammar and Usage | I
Which sentence uses the verb correctly after the word neither?
= Neither of the boys has finished his homework.
- Neither of the boys have finished his homework.
- Neither of the boys are finished his homework.
- Neither of the boys were finishing his homework.
? Neither is singular, so it takes has.

@ Grammar and Usage | I
Which sentence uses the correct verb form with the phrase the number of?
= The number of students is increasing.
- The number of students are increasing.
- The number of students were increasing.
- The number of student are increasing.
? The subject is the singular noun number, so the verb is singular.

@ Grammar and Usage | I
Which part of the sentence contains an error? "Each of the players (A) have received (B) a medal (C) from the captain (D)."
= B
- A
- C
- D
? Each is singular, so it should be has received.

@ Grammar and Usage | I
Choose the correct preposition: He is very good ___ mathematics.
= at
- in
- on
- with
? The standard collocation is good at.

@ Grammar and Usage | A
Choose the correct word: Hardly had he reached the station ___ the train left.
= when
- than
- then
- while
? Hardly is followed by when, while no sooner is followed by than.

@ Grammar and Usage | A
Which sentence uses its correctly?
= The dog wagged its tail.
- The dog wagged it's tail.
- The dog wagged its' tail.
- The dog wagged it tail.
? Its is the possessive form. It's is the short form of it is or it has.

@ Grammar and Usage | B
What is the past tense of the verb choose?
= Chose
- Choosed
- Chosen
- Choosen
? Choose, chose, chosen are the three forms. Chosen is the past participle.

@ Grammar and Usage | I
Which word completes the sentence correctly? There are ___ students in the class today than yesterday.
= fewer
- less
- lesser
- little
? Use fewer with countable nouns and less with uncountable nouns.

@ Grammar and Usage | I
Choose the correct spelling.
= Necessary
- Neccessary
- Necesary
- Nesessary
? Necessary has one c and two s.

@ Grammar and Usage | I
What is the plural of the word criterion?
= Criteria
- Criterions
- Criterias
- Criteriae
? Criterion comes from Greek and its plural is criteria.

@ Fill in the Blanks | B
She is ___ honest girl.
= an
- a
- the
- one
? Honest begins with a vowel sound, so the article is an.

@ Fill in the Blanks | B
The sun ___ in the east.
= rises
- rise
- rising
- raise
? A general truth uses the simple present, and sun is singular.

@ Fill in the Blanks | I
He insisted ___ paying the bill.
= on
- at
- to
- for
? The verb insist is followed by on.

@ Fill in the Blanks | I
I have been waiting ___ two hours.
= for
- since
- from
- during
? Use for with a duration and since with a starting point in time.

@ Fill in the Blanks | I
If I ___ you, I would apologise.
= were
- am
- was
- be
? In a hypothetical condition the subjunctive form were is used.

@ Fill in the Blanks | I
One of my friends ___ a doctor.
= is
- are
- were
- be
? The subject is the singular word one, not friends.

@ Fill in the Blanks | I
Despite ___ hard, she failed the exam.
= studying
- study
- studied
- to study
? Despite is followed by a noun or a gerund.

@ Idioms and Phrases | B
What does the idiom "a piece of cake" mean?
= Something very easy
- A small dessert
- A difficult problem
- A gift
? The phrase describes a task that is easy to do.

@ Idioms and Phrases | B
What does the idiom "break the ice" mean?
= To start a conversation in an awkward situation
- To damage something
- To stop a meeting
- To make a cold drink
? It refers to easing tension when people meet for the first time.

@ Idioms and Phrases | I
What does the idiom "once in a blue moon" mean?
= Very rarely
- Every month
- At night
- Very frequently
? A blue moon is a rare event, so the phrase means almost never.

@ Idioms and Phrases | I
What does the idiom "hit the nail on the head" mean?
= To say or do exactly the right thing
- To injure oneself
- To build a house
- To make a mistake
? It means to be exactly correct about something.

@ Idioms and Phrases | I
What does the idiom "under the weather" mean?
= Feeling slightly ill
- Standing in the rain
- Being very happy
- Traveling abroad
? The phrase is used when someone feels unwell.

@ Idioms and Phrases | I
What does the idiom "burn the midnight oil" mean?
= To work late into the night
- To waste fuel
- To cook at night
- To start a fire
? It comes from the time when people used oil lamps to work after dark.

@ One-word Substitution | B
Choose one word for: a person who writes books.
= Author
- Editor
- Reader
- Publisher
? An author is the writer of a book or article.

@ One-word Substitution | I
Choose one word for: a person who loves mankind and helps others through donations.
= Philanthropist
- Misanthrope
- Pessimist
- Egoist
? A philanthropist works to promote the welfare of others, often by giving money.

@ One-word Substitution | I
Choose one word for: a person who cannot read or write.
= Illiterate
- Ignorant
- Amateur
- Novice
? Illiterate describes someone without the ability to read and write.

@ One-word Substitution | I
Choose one word for: the scientific study of birds.
= Ornithology
- Etymology
- Entomology
- Geology
? Ornithology is the study of birds. Entomology is the study of insects.

@ One-word Substitution | A
Choose one word for: a person who knows everything.
= Omniscient
- Omnipotent
- Omnivorous
- Omnipresent
? Omniscient means all-knowing. Omnipotent means all-powerful and omnipresent means present everywhere.

@ Reading Comprehension | I
Read the passage. "Regular exercise improves heart health, lifts mood and helps maintain a healthy weight. However, doing too much without rest can lead to injury." What is the main idea?
= Exercise has benefits but should be balanced with rest
- Exercise should be avoided
- Rest is more important than exercise
- Only weight matters in exercise
? The passage lists benefits and then warns that too much exercise without rest is risky.

@ Reading Comprehension | I
Read the passage. "The library extended its opening hours during exams so that students could study late." Why were the hours extended?
= To let students study late during exams
- To save electricity
- To hire more staff
- To close the library earlier
? The passage states the reason directly: students could study late.

@ Reading Comprehension | I
Read the passage. "Although the new phone is expensive, its battery lasts three days, which many users find worth the price." What can be inferred?
= Some users feel the battery life justifies the cost
- The phone is cheap
- The battery is poor
- No one buys the phone
? The passage says many users find the long battery life worth the price.

@ Reading Comprehension | A
Read the passage. "Renewable energy is not free of drawbacks: solar panels need mined materials and wind farms can affect wildlife. Even so, their lifetime emissions are far lower than those of coal." The author's attitude is best described as:
= Balanced and fact-based
- Strongly opposed to renewables
- Uncritically enthusiastic
- Indifferent
? The author admits drawbacks and still concludes that emissions are lower, which is a balanced view.

@ Reading Comprehension | A
Read the passage. "Rahul never missed a deadline in five years, yet he was overlooked for promotion." Which statement is implied?
= Meeting deadlines alone may not guarantee a promotion
- Rahul missed deadlines
- Promotions depend only on deadlines
- Rahul did not work for five years
? The word yet signals a contrast between good performance and the outcome.

@ Reading Comprehension | I
Read the passage. "Heavy rain flooded the roads, so buses were delayed." What caused the delay?
= Flooded roads caused by heavy rain
- Bus driver strike
- Fuel shortage
- A holiday
? The word so links the cause (flooded roads) to the effect (delayed buses).

@ Sentence Transformation | I
Choose the passive voice of: The chef cooked the meal.
= The meal was cooked by the chef.
- The meal is cooked by the chef.
- The chef was cooked by the meal.
- The meal has cooked the chef.
? The simple past active becomes was or were plus the past participle in the passive.

@ Sentence Transformation | I
Choose the correct indirect speech for: He said, "I am tired."
= He said that he was tired.
- He said that I am tired.
- He said that he is tired yesterday.
- He said he will be tired.
? In reported speech the present tense usually shifts to the past, and the pronoun changes.

@ Grammar and Usage | B
Which of these words is a noun?
= Happiness
- Quickly
- Beautiful
- Run
? Happiness names a quality or state, so it is an abstract noun.
